ack of a garbage wagon for  the crew of alle in 1932 barry kern  credits his father with the vision that  brought the family business to the level  it's at today and my dad Blaine Kern who  they now called mr. Mardi Gras here in  town was a little boy and remembers  working with his dad and helping him out  and always helped his dad to build the  owl a parade over 50 parades filled the  streets of the Big Easy attracting  hundreds of thousands of people over the  to month-long celebration the floats are  paid for by the members of the cruise  these crews are like fraternities or  sororities or clubs and everybody's part  of these different crews and they pay to  be members to got probably 60,000  members of these carnival organizations  for 44 years mardi gras historian Arthur  Hardy has published the definitive  annual guide to carnival in New Orleans  we sat down over cafe brew lo or Devils  coffee at legendary Antoine's restaurant  what does Mardi Gras mean to you  personally it's the people you know it's  the feeling it's the Brotherhood in the  street everybody's equal at Mardi Gras  it doesn't matter color age you know  we're all coming together just to have a  good time it's what we do and when did  floats come into the picture really not  until the first real official Mardi Gras  parade in 1857 we started out with one  parade with two floats now in our Metro  era we have nearly 60 parades more than  1,100 floats it's probably the largest  celebration of its kind anywhere and in  the spirit of a celebration thrown for  the people by the people Hardy says  there's one thing you'll never see on a  Mardi Gras float in NOAA and one of the  most amazing things about it is that  it's not commercially sponsored the  shareholders of Mardi Gras the citizens  of New Orleans and it's actually a law  in New Orleans that you can't have  commercial signs or you know Pepsi on  the side of a float can't do it the  current party and everyone who was in  the city at the time there was one  particular year when the sight of the  floats rolling through the streets was  especially meaningful I remember  standing at those parades only months  after Katrina  and having people with tears rolling  down their faces what did that year mean  to you to get all Mardi Gras crews  running again just months after Katrina  when you literally have thousands of  people died tens of thousand people out  of their homes no one was thinking about  Mardi Gras so we knew that it was very  very important to have those parades for  many reasons I think that when we did it  and it happened I think everybody  understood why it needed to happen and  as New Orleans continues to build  rebuild and grow it's clear that the one  thing that weathers all storms is the  spirit of the people that call this  place home  I think genetically encoded in our DNA  this is how we celebrate it's joyful its  joyful and if if other people don't get  it that's their problem but we're gonna  do it forever I don't think we need to  justify let's just go celebrate and you  better believe it I'll see you in the  streets I'll see you in the streets  I should mention the young man you saw  walking next to Barry Kernan is his son  the fourth generation Fitz Kern and that  waiter preparing that great cafe brew  Lowe was Charles Carter one of the best  lawyers in the world at outed Antoine's  you know every one of them don't you and  you do too you know they know you by  name oh come on they know me for some  other reason that is not true but being  in New Orleans with you or some of the  best times I've ever had we have a good  time down there yeah whether were Bolin  or rock and roll

